
Can AI help us map real-world flood-affected areas using satellite imagery?

In this, I have explored AI-assisted flood mapping for the 26 August 2026 flood in the Trishuli River corridor, Nepal, using Google Earth Engine (GEE), Sentinel-1 SAR, Sentinel-2 optical imagery, GEE AI, and ChatGPT.

1) Sentinel-1 pre- and post-flood backscatter change detection (SAR)
2) Sentinel-2 spectral index analysis using MNDWI, NDVI, and Cloud Score+
3) Comparing AI-assisted workflows and inspecting flood inundation extents

Limitations: Different prompts, thresholds, and sensor methodologies yield different flood extents. The mapped outputs require scientific interpretation and independent validation before operational use.



## Prompts used for the analysis in GEE AI

Select suitable satellite data
Prompt 1 —  Which satellite data is most suitable for flood analyzing the 26 August 2026 flood in the Trishuli River corridor, Rasuwa, Nepal? 

Sentinel-1 SAR change detection and potential flood affected area mapping
Prompt 2 — Use Sentinel-1 SAR imagery for the Trishuli corridor, Nepal, to analyze the effects of the 26 August 2026 flood using an established pre/post-flood change-detection approach. Show the pre-flood image, post-flood image, backscatter change, and flood layers. Keep the analysis for locally justified thresholds for the Sentinel-1 imagery.

Sentinel-2 Cloud Score+ dataset for validation
Prompt 3 —  Display low-cloud Sentinel-2 Cloud Score+ images from before and after the 26 August 2026 flood to validate the flood-affected areas

Sentinel-2 multi-index approach and potential flood affected area mapping
Prompt 4 — Sentinel-2 with Cloud Score+ for flood affected layer and Keep the Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 analyses completely separate and show the final flood affected layer for comparison.

Improve Sentinel-2 using multiple indices
Prompt 5 — Improve the Sentinel-2 flood analysis using an established multi-index approach with MNDWI, NDVI,  instead of MNDWI alone. Keep Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 results separate, and calculate the potential affected area in hectares and km² for each.
